WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.740
Think that Donald Trump is a better president
than Abraham Lincoln.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:10.540
There is a new economist you gov Paul from
right around Thanksgiving and it's a normal

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:12.200
poll where they ask about many things.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:17.930
They ask about presidential approval, opinions
of items in the news, but then it has something

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:23.500
very interesting and it asks how do you see
Trump versus past presidents?

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.350
And this is a real insight into Trumpist minds.

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:30.480
And it also relates to some cognitive biases
that are important to understand.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.840
So this will be useful.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.550
Voters were asked which Republican was a better
president, Donald Trump or Abraham Lincoln?

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:41.970
Most demographic groups, no matter how you
slice it, we're pretty evenly United that

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.120
Lincoln was a better president.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.700
If you look at men and women, men and women,
roughly equally mid seventies to mid twenties

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.829
believe Lincoln was a better president, okay?

00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:55.930
When you look at different income groups,
people earning under 50,000 between 50 and

00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:00.350
a hundred thousand and more than a hundred
thousand roughly by the same three to one

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:04.969
margin, they view Lincoln as the better president
than Donald Trump.

00:01:04.969 --> 00:01:11.399
But when you look at party identification,
Republicans by 53 to 47 margin believe that

00:01:11.399 --> 00:01:15.340
Donald Trump is a better president than Abraham
Lincoln.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.899
Now, as a side note, before we continue, Republicans
also think Trump is a way better president

00:01:20.899 --> 00:01:25.219
than George W. Bush by 71 to 29 margin, which
is stunning.

00:01:25.219 --> 00:01:28.560
And again, George W. Bush was no angel, truly,
but Trump.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:29.570
Incredible.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:33.189
But let's discuss what's going on here because
there's evidence of a few different things.

00:01:33.189 --> 00:01:41.060
Number one, there is a blinding ideology for
some where they truly see Trump as the best

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.609
without applying any real critical thinking
to the question of Lincoln versus Trump.

00:01:44.609 --> 00:01:46.549
They just say, Trump's better.

00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:48.229
For others it's ignorance.

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:53.409
They don't really know what Lincoln did, so
Trump is better in their minds.

00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:57.579
It's a combination of ignorance plus lack
of critical thinking.

00:01:57.579 --> 00:02:01.859
For others, they actually don't like Lincoln
because he freed the slaves and it was on

00:02:01.859 --> 00:02:03.850
the side of the union in the civil war.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:07.829
You can see this when you look at the regionalized
results, we're in the South.

00:02:07.829 --> 00:02:13.099
The numbers are most skewed against Lincoln
and towards Trump and then there are just

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:17.070
some sort of general cognitive biases at play.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.659
There's the recency bias.

00:02:18.659 --> 00:02:24.450
That's where a person more easily remembers
recent events compared to something that occurred

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:27.520
a while back that will skew results.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:32.730
This would suggest that any poll about the
current president versus a president hundreds

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:39.020
of years ago would be skewed towards the current
president to some degree because of recency

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:40.020
bias.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.010
Then you also have the availability bias,
which is kind of implicit in what I already

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:44.010
told you.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:50.900
This is the human tendency to think of examples
more easily of things that happened recently

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:55.879
and to imagine that they are representative
in a group to a greater degree than they are.

00:02:55.879 --> 00:03:02.269
So if you can think of more things Trump did
that you like compared to Lincoln, you extrapolate

00:03:02.269 --> 00:03:06.239
and you say, well, Trump is better on that
basis because I can remember more things that

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:07.430
Donald Trump did.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:09.159
So this is about many things.

00:03:09.159 --> 00:03:13.470
It is in part about the ideologically blinded,
ignorant Republicans.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.129
It's also about the cult of personality around
Donald Trump.

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:19.609
But there are some general cognitive biases
at play as well.

00:03:19.609 --> 00:03:25.260
What I believe this points to as a strategy,
and I know I'm a broken record on this, you

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:30.629
can't argue with people who think Trump is
better than Lincoln and rationally explain

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:33.459
to them why they should vote for someone else.

